congressman and civil rights activist  John Lewis says his cancer diagnosis his  one more fight he will take on  correspondent Jonathan Serrie has the  story as an icon of the civil rights  movement Democrat John Lewis has never  shied from a challenge now fighting for  his life  Lewis plans to continue representing  Georgia's fifth congressional district  while undergoing treatment for Stage  four pancreatic cancer  he explains while I'm clear-eyed about  the prognosis doctors have told me that  recent medical advances have made this  type of cancer treatable in many cases  the treatment options are no longer as  debilitating as they once were and that  I have a fighting chance former  President Barack Obama tweeted if  there's one thing I love about  representative John Lewis  it's his incomparable will to fight I  know he's got a lot more of that left in  him a fighter is how colleagues on both  sides of the aisle describe Lewis who in  2016 led Democrats in a surprise sit-in  in the House chamber to call for gun  control two decades before his election  to Congress in 1986 Lewis suffered a  fractured skull in the bloody sunday  march across Selma Alabama Edmund Pettus  Bridge which helped galvanize support  for the Voting Rights Act of 1965  congressman Lewis showed great love in  the face of violence and brutality and  so we are praying with great compassion  for congressman Lewis the warrior I'll  always remember him as the gentle yet  very strong warrior North Carolina  Republican Mark Walker tweeted a photo  of himself and Lewis at a recent  commemoration of the Selma March rioting  it was one of the greatest honors of my  life to walk by his side across this  bridge and I will do the same as he  crosses his next Lewis said he may have  to miss some House votes while  undergoing cancer treatment over the  next several weeks but with prayers and  God's grace he hopes to be back on the  frontline soon Mike Jonathan thank you  very much
 

